// ============================================================ 7. MAPREDUCE DESIGN
const s7 = [
  H1('7. MapReduce Design'),
  P('In SQL you write a GROUP BY clause. In MapReduce you do not: you choose what the mapper emits as its key, and the framework’s Shuffle-and-Sort stage performs the grouping for you, in parallel, with no shared state and no coordination between tasks. Every analysis in this project is a different answer to one question — what should the key be?'),
  H2('7.1 The key designs'),
  table(['Analysis', 'Map output key', 'Why that key'], [
    ['(a) Hourly', '"14" (hour, zero-padded)', 'Only 24 keys. Padding is mandatory: Hadoop sorts keys as TEXT, so "9" would sort after "23".'],
    ['(b) Daily', '"3_Wednesday_WEEKDAY"', 'An ordinal prefix forces calendar order out of a lexicographic sort; the suffix lets one job answer both the day and the weekday/weekend question.'],
    ['(c) Locations', '"237" (3-digit zone id)', 'One key per zone. Ranking is deliberately not done here — see 7.3.'],
    ['(d) Revenue', '"237" (3-digit zone id)', 'Same grouping, richer value: a numeric vector rather than a count.'],
    ['(e) Payment', '"1_CreditCard"', 'Code plus label, so the output is readable without a join.'],
    ['(f) Distance', '"3_5-10mi"', 'Binning happens in the MAPPER, turning a continuous variable into 5 keys before the shuffle.'],
    ['(g) Routes', '"132->230"', 'A COMPOSITE key — a two-column GROUP BY expressed as string concatenation. This is the whole analysis.'],
    ['(h) Duration', '"4_20-30min"', 'Duration does not exist in the source; it is derived map-side from the two timestamps.'],
    ['(i) Anomalies', 'the anomaly label', 'One record may emit several keys, plus two bookkeeping totals prefixed AAA_/AAB_ so they sort first.'],
    ['Cleaning', 'the ENTIRE record', 'Identical records collide in the shuffle — a distributed DISTINCT.'],
    ['Top-N', 'the constant "TOPN"', 'Deliberately forces one reducer: the only place a GLOBAL ordering can exist.'],
  ], [16, 26, 58]),
  H2('7.2 What Shuffle and Sort actually does'),
  P('Between map and reduce, the framework performs four steps that the programmer never writes:'),
  bulletRich([['Partition. ', {bold: true}], ['Each map output key is hashed and assigned to a reducer — by default hash(key) mod numReduceTasks. All records with the same key therefore land on the same reducer, which is the guarantee the whole model rests on.']]),
  bulletRich([['Sort. ', {bold: true}], ['Each map task sorts its output by key before writing it, spilling to local disk when the sort buffer fills.']]),
  bulletRich([['Copy. ', {bold: true}], ['Each reducer fetches its partition from every map task over HTTP — this is the network-expensive step, and the one a combiner attacks.']]),
  bulletRich([['Merge. ', {bold: true}], ['The reducer merge-sorts the fetched runs so that all values for one key arrive contiguously.']]),
  P('The last point is why every reducer in this project is written as a streaming loop that holds one accumulator and watches for the key to change. It never builds a dictionary of all keys. Memory is constant in the number of groups, which is what lets a single reducer aggregate ' + n(D.n_routes) + ' routes without difficulty.'),
  H2('7.3 Why ranking is not done in the reducer'),
  P('A mapper sees only its own input split; a reducer sees only the keys in its own partition. Neither can know a global ranking. The Top-10 zones and Top-20 routes are therefore computed by a second MapReduce job that reads the first job’s HDFS output — which is exactly the multi-stage workflow of Section 10, and is the honest reason it exists rather than a contrived one.'),
  H2('7.4 The combiner, and the measured effect'),
  P('A combiner is a reducer that Hadoop may run map-side, on one map task’s output, before the shuffle. It is legal only when the aggregation is commutative and associative and when its output has the same shape as its input.'),
  RP([['This is precisely why no mapper in this project emits an average. ', {}],
      ['An average is not associative', {bold: true}],
      [' — the mean of two means is not the mean of the union unless the group sizes are equal. So the mappers emit fixed-length vectors of the form (count, sum, sum, ...) and the reducer divides at the very end. That single decision makes one generic combiner, ', {}],
      ['combiner_sums.py', {code: true}],
      [', legal for five different analyses: revenue, payment, distance, duration and routes.', {}]]),
  P('The effect is measurable in the job counters. For the route analysis — the worst case, with ' + n(D.n_routes) + ' distinct keys:'),
  table(['Counter', 'Value', 'Meaning'], [
    ['Map output records', n(routeJob.map_out), 'One record per trip, emitted by the mappers'],
    ['Combine input records', n(routeCombIn), 'Fed to the map-side combiner'],
    ['Combine output records', n(routeCombOut), 'What actually crossed the shuffle'],
    ['Reduce input records', n(routeJob.red_in), 'Received by the reducers'],
    ['Reduction factor', (routeCombIn / Math.max(routeCombOut, 1)).toFixed(1) + 'x', 'Shuffle traffic avoided'],
  ], [30, 22, 48], {right: [1]}),
  caption('Table 7.1 — Combiner effect on job 08-BusiestPickupDropoffRoutes (' + routeJob.app + ').'),
  P('On a single-node cluster this is the difference between a job that spills to disk repeatedly and one that does not. On a real cluster it is the difference between saturating the network and not.'),
  H2('7.5 Choosing the number of reducers'),
  P('Most jobs here run with one reducer, because after combining, the reduce input is tiny — at most a few tens of thousands of records. The route job uses two, and the cleaning job uses two because its shuffle is a full gigabyte that genuinely benefits from being split. The Top-N jobs must use exactly one: with two reducers there would be two partial rankings and no global answer, which is the trade-off the Top-N pattern makes deliberately.'),
];

