// ============================================================ 12. PERFORMANCE
const pandasFull = 13.52, pandasChunk = 10.96, pandasPeak = 1347.4, pandasChunkPeak = 200.2;
const hadoopFixed = tinyJob.elapsed;
const marginalHadoop = ((benchJob.elapsed - hadoopFixed) / (VALID / 1e6)).toFixed(1);
const marginalPandas = ((pandasChunk - 0.15) / (VALID / 1e6)).toFixed(2);
const s12 = [
  H1('12. Performance Comparison'),
  P('The comparison uses one analysis run two ways: revenue by pickup zone — trip count, total fare, total tips, total revenue, average fare and average distance, grouped by PULocationID — over the identical ' + n(VALID) + ' cleaned records. On the Hadoop side that is mapper_revenue.py with combiner_sums.py and reducer_revenue.py. On the Pandas side it is a groupby over the same columns. Both produce the same ' + D.n_zones + ' groups.'),
  H2('12.1 The headline table'),
  table(['Metric', 'Python / Pandas', 'Hadoop MapReduce'], [
    ['Dataset size', '1.03 GB (2 CSV part files)', '1.03 GB in HDFS (' + benchJob.maps + ' blocks)'],
    ['Number of records', n(VALID), n(VALID)],
    ['Execution time', pandasChunk.toFixed(2) + ' s chunked / ' + pandasFull.toFixed(2) + ' s full load',
                       benchJob.elapsed + ' s'],
    ['Memory used', n(pandasChunkPeak, 0) + ' MB chunked / ' + n(pandasPeak, 0) + ' MB full load',
                    n((benchJob.peak_map_mem || 0) / 1048576, 0) + ' MB peak map, ' +
                    n((benchJob.peak_red_mem || 0) / 1048576, 0) + ' MB peak reduce'],
    ['Mapper tasks', '— (single process)', String(benchJob.maps)],
    ['Reducer tasks', '—', String(benchJob.reds)],
    ['Output size', D.n_zones + ' rows', D.n_zones + ' rows'],
    ['Parallelism', '1 thread', String(benchJob.maps) + ' map tasks, up to 4 concurrent containers'],
    ['CPU time (framework-reported)', '—', n((benchJob.cpu_ms || 0) / 1000, 1) + ' s'],
  ], [24, 38, 38]),
  caption('Table 12.1 — The required comparison table. Hadoop job ' + benchJob.app + ', run with nothing else on the cluster.'),
  RP([['Pandas is ', {}], [(benchJob.elapsed / pandasChunk).toFixed(0) + ' times faster', {bold: true}],
      [' than Hadoop on this dataset, on this machine. That is the measured result, and it is also the ', {}],
      ['expected', {italics: true}], [' result. Reporting it honestly is more useful than engineering a comparison that flatters Hadoop.', {}]]),
  H2('12.2 Why — a three-point scaling measurement'),
  P('A single data point cannot distinguish "Hadoop is slow" from "Hadoop has fixed overhead". So the identical analysis was run at three scales on both systems.'),
  table(['Scale', 'Records', 'Hadoop', 'Pandas (chunked)', 'Ratio'], [
    ['Tiny', n(100000), tinyJob.elapsed + ' s', '0.15 s', (tinyJob.elapsed / 0.15).toFixed(0) + 'x'],
    ['Half', n(5248143), halfJob.elapsed + ' s', '6.00 s', (halfJob.elapsed / 6.0).toFixed(0) + 'x'],
    ['Full', n(VALID), benchJob.elapsed + ' s', pandasChunk.toFixed(2) + ' s',
            (benchJob.elapsed / pandasChunk).toFixed(0) + 'x'],
  ], [16, 22, 20, 24, 18], {right: [1, 2, 3, 4]}),
  caption('Table 12.2 — Scaling behaviour. The ratio narrows as the data grows, which is the whole argument.'),
  P('Two numbers fall straight out of this table.'),
  bulletRich([['Hadoop’s fixed overhead is about ' + hadoopFixed + ' seconds. ', {bold: true}],
    ['A job over 100,000 records — essentially no work — still takes ' + hadoopFixed + ' s. That is the cost of submitting to the ResourceManager, allocating and launching an ApplicationMaster container, launching map and reduce JVMs, running the shuffle machinery and committing output. It is paid in full whether the input is 10 MB or 10 TB.']]),
  bulletRich([['Hadoop’s marginal cost is about ' + marginalHadoop + ' seconds per million records ', {bold: true}],
    ['on this 2-core node, against roughly ' + marginalPandas + ' s per million for Pandas. Per record, in a single process, Pandas is genuinely faster — its aggregation runs in vectorised C over typed NumPy arrays, while every Hadoop record is serialised to text, parsed by a Python process, written to a sort buffer and read back.']]),
  P('Notice, though, that the ratio falls from ' + (tinyJob.elapsed / 0.15).toFixed(0) + ' times to ' + (benchJob.elapsed / pandasChunk).toFixed(0) + ' times across two orders of magnitude of data. The gap is closing, and it closes because the fixed cost is being amortised.'),
  H2('12.3 Where the crossover actually is'),
  P('Speed is the wrong axis on which to make the decision. The right one is the ceiling, and the ceiling is visible in the memory column.'),
  P('The full-load Pandas variant — the one a data scientist writes first — peaked at ' + n(pandasPeak, 0) + ' MB of resident memory for 1.03 GB of CSV, a factor of about ' + (pandasPeak / 1024 / 1.03).toFixed(2) + '. On this 8 GB machine that hits the wall at roughly 6 GB of CSV, or about 60 million trip records, which is a bit over one year of yellow-taxi data. Add the green-taxi and for-hire-vehicle feeds, or ask for two years, and the program does not get slower; it fails.'),
  P('The chunked Pandas variant avoids that, holding a bounded ' + n(pandasChunkPeak, 0) + ' MB by streaming the file and accumulating partial group sums. But look at what it is: a hand-written, single-threaded MapReduce. It has a map step (per-chunk groupby), a combine step (partial aggregation) and a reduce step (concatenate and sum by level). Writing it means reimplementing, badly and without fault tolerance, the thing Hadoop already provides — and it is still pinned to one core on one machine.'),
  P('Hadoop’s ' + marginalHadoop + ' s per million is a per-node figure. It is divided by the number of nodes. Ten machines would put this job near the ' + hadoopFixed + '-second floor; Pandas on ten machines is still Pandas on one machine, because there is no mechanism in it that uses the other nine.'),
  H2('12.4 The decision rule'),
  table(['Use', 'When'], [
    ['Pandas / single machine', 'The data fits comfortably in RAM with room for intermediates — in practice under a few GB. Interactive, exploratory, iterative work. Fastest path to an answer, by a wide margin.'],
    ['Hadoop MapReduce', 'The data exceeds one machine’s memory or disk; the job must survive node failure without restarting from scratch; the work is a scheduled batch pass over historical data where a fixed one-minute overhead is irrelevant; or the volume is growing and the architecture must scale by adding machines rather than by buying a bigger one.'],
  ], [26, 74]),
  P('For this specific quarter of taxi data on this specific machine, Pandas is the correct engineering choice, and the honest conclusion of the benchmark is to say so. The reason to build the Hadoop pipeline is that the same code, unchanged, runs against ten years of data on a fifty-node cluster — and the Pandas script does not run against it at all.'),
  P('One caveat in Hadoop’s favour that Table 12.1 understates: the cleaning job (Section 6) shuffled a full gigabyte to deduplicate ' + n(RAW) + ' records. A Pandas equivalent would need every record resident simultaneously to detect duplicates, at roughly 1.3 GB per gigabyte of input. The distributed version has no such requirement, because the deduplication happens inside a sort that is allowed to spill to disk.'),
];

