// ============================================================ 15. CONCLUSION
const s15 = [
  H1('15. Conclusion'),
  P('A complete Hadoop analytics pipeline was designed, implemented and executed over ' + n(RAW) + ' NYC yellow-taxi trip records. HDFS stored the 1.1 GB dataset across ten 128 MB blocks; a two-phase MapReduce job validated and deduplicated it down to ' + n(VALID) + ' analysable records; nine analytical jobs and five Top-N jobs, all written as Python programs run through Hadoop Streaming, produced the results in Section 9; and YARN scheduled all ' + jobs.length + ' applications, every one of which succeeded.'),
  P('The analysis itself produced three findings a trip-count summary would have missed. Demand is a broad afternoon-to-evening plateau rather than a commuter peak, and the weekend is busier per day than the weekday once the totals are normalised. Revenue and volume point at different parts of the city — the two airports produce ' + pct(airportRev / D.revenue_total * 100, 1) + ' of revenue from a small fraction of trips. And only ' + D.routes_overlap.length + ' of the top twenty routes by frequency also appear in the top twenty by revenue, which means any dispatch policy tuned on trip counts is optimising the wrong quantity.'),
  P('On the technical side, the exercise made three things concrete that are easy to state and hard to feel. The choice of map output key is the algorithm — a composite key gives a two-column GROUP BY, the whole record as key gives a distributed DISTINCT, and a constant key gives a global ranking at the cost of a serial reduce. Averages are not associative, which is why every mapper here emits sums and counts and why one generic combiner could serve five different analyses, cutting the route job’s shuffle by a factor of ' + (routeCombIn / Math.max(routeCombOut, 1)).toFixed(0) + '. And ranking genuinely cannot be done in a single job, which is why the multi-stage workflow exists for a reason rather than for a mark.'),
  P('The benchmark is the part worth being blunt about. Pandas beat Hadoop by a factor of ' + (benchJob.elapsed / pandasChunk).toFixed(0) + ' on this dataset, and for a single quarter of taxi data on a single machine, Pandas is the right tool. Hadoop’s ' + hadoopFixed + '-second fixed overhead is unrecoverable at this scale. What the three-point scaling measurement shows is where that stops being true: the ratio narrows as data grows, Pandas’ memory ceiling arrives at roughly one year of this feed on an 8 GB machine, and Hadoop’s per-record cost is the only one of the two that divides by the number of nodes. The pipeline built here would run unchanged over ten years of data on fifty machines. The Pandas script would not run over it at all.'),
  P('That is the real lesson of the assignment, and it is not "Hadoop is fast". It is that the interesting property of a distributed system is not its speed on data that fits, but its behaviour on data that does not.'),
];

